I should optimize `usersOf`. The current implementation recomputes `refs` for all members *per target* (26 times!) and the fixpoint is O(|A|) iterations.

Optimization: compute ancestors via **reverse BFS**: build reverse edges once per mode (each member → its refs; invert), then BFS from target. That's O(members × refs) once per mode + BFS O(edges). Much faster.

Implementation in the UseProbe template:
```lean
def usersOfAll (env) (members : Array Name) (targets : Array Name) (valuesOnly : Bool) :
    Std.HashMap Name (Array Name) := Id.run do
  -- forward refs for members
  let mut refs : Std.HashMap Name (Array Name) := {}
  for n in members do refs := refs.insert n (directRefs env n valuesOnly)
  -- reverse adjacency: only for members that appear as refs (plus all members)
  let mut rev : Std.HashMap Name (Array Name) := {}
  for n in members do
    for c in refs.getD n #[] do
      rev := rev.insert c ((rev.getD c #[]).push n)
  -- BFS per target
  let mut out : Std.HashMap Name (Array Name) := {}
  for t in targets do
    let mut seen : Std.HashSet Name := {t}
    let mut work : Array Name := #[t]
    while !work.isEmpty do
      let x := work.back!; work := work.pop
      for p in rev.getD x #[] do
        if !seen.contains p then seen := seen.insert p; work := work.push p
    out := out.insert t (seen.erase t).toArray.qsort ...
  return out
```
Memory: rev map has one entry per (member, ref) pair — for 537 members × ~100 refs = 54k entries, fine.

